WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.588
Two tasks write the part of a reinforcement-learning

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:05.991
loop that turns what a model did into

3
00:00:06.055 --> 00:00:06.940
training data.

4
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.090
Mistakes here do not crash anything.

5
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.840
They quietly teach the wrong thing.

6
00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.346
First, RAGEN, which trains language models

7
00:00:16.422 --> 00:00:18.150
to act over many turns.

8
00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.386
The model sees a puzzle, here Sokoban

9
00:00:21.386 --> 00:00:22.784
replies with moves

10
00:00:22.784 --> 00:00:25.370
and gets the next view and a reward.

11
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.760
Each game is packed into one row of tokens.

12
00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.241
A mask marks what the model wrote

13
00:00:31.241 --> 00:00:33.622
because the loss is taken only there

14
00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:35.314
and the reward goes on the

15
00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:36.630
last token it wrote.

16
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.537
Four games from the same start form a group

17
00:00:39.595 --> 00:00:41.455
and each reward is compared with

18
00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:43.140
the group's mean and spread.

19
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.008
In our toy, the solved game stands out

20
00:00:46.074 --> 00:00:47.730
at one point seven three.

21
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.251
A group that all failed the same

22
00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:52.015
way gets zero everywhere

23
00:00:52.015 --> 00:00:53.440
and teaches nothing.

24
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.660
The task removes this context manager.

25
00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.232
One training step runs six groups of

26
00:00:59.293 --> 00:01:01.453
four games with a small Qwen model

27
00:01:01.515 --> 00:01:03.922
and every group must beat the reference

28
00:01:03.984 --> 00:01:05.650
on both success and reward.

29
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.476
The reference solved no game at all

30
00:01:08.476 --> 00:01:09.920
and no model passed.

31
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.736
Opus noticed that the small model copies

32
00:01:12.798 --> 00:01:14.771
whatever earlier answer it sees

33
00:01:14.771 --> 00:01:16.127
broken ones included

34
00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:18.285
so it keeps the previous turn only

35
00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:19.950
when its moves were valid.

36
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.922
It shared the best score

37
00:01:21.922 --> 00:01:23.284
eighteen point five

38
00:01:23.284 --> 00:01:25.100
against eleven point seven.

39
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.518
The second task, Flow-OPD

40
00:01:27.589 --> 00:01:29.851
teaches an image model to render

41
00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:31.760
the words a prompt quotes.

42
00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.846
The student draws its own

43
00:01:33.915 --> 00:01:36.970
chain of latent states, from noise to image

44
00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:38.983
and a teacher tuned for text

45
00:01:39.052 --> 00:01:40.510
supervises each step.

46
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.634
Each step is a Gaussian

47
00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:44.704
the velocity sets its mean

48
00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.700
and noise sets its spread.

49
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.595
Training replays every recorded step

50
00:01:49.664 --> 00:01:51.727
so the step must give the same

51
00:01:51.796 --> 00:01:54.410
log-probability again, almost exactly.

52
00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.150
How much can one step teach?

53
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.252
Near pure noise the spread is wide

54
00:02:00.252 --> 00:02:02.060
so a step says little.

55
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:03.873
In our ten-step schedule

56
00:02:03.932 --> 00:02:06.389
the first step carries about a hundred and

57
00:02:06.448 --> 00:02:08.730
thirty times less signal than the last.

58
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.960
Capping the noise raises the early steps.

59
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.089
Pause and predict.

60
00:02:14.176 --> 00:02:16.327
Claude Opus 5.5 put a cap

61
00:02:16.414 --> 00:02:19.340
on the early noise, as in our toy.

62
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.210
Did it beat the reference?

63
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.560
No.

64
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.023
It scored fifty-five point nine

65
00:02:28.023 --> 00:02:30.070
against sixty-one point eight.

66
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.980
Ten of sixteen models did beat it.

67
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.527
Step 5 Preview won seven of the

68
00:02:35.598 --> 00:02:38.503
eight hidden workloads, and still failed

69
00:02:38.503 --> 00:02:40.770
because a pass needs all eight.

70
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.560
No model passed.

71
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.502
So: put the mask on the model's own tokens

72
00:02:46.502 --> 00:02:48.765
compare games within their group

73
00:02:48.832 --> 00:02:50.562
and make every step replay

74
00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:51.960
to a tiny tolerance.

75
00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.670
Mistakes in any of these fail silently.

76
00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.057
Next: losses over whole populations

77
00:02:59.057 --> 00:03:00.660
and a world model.
